Under DORA, contractual arrangements with ICT third-party service providers must be set out clearly in writing, with the rights and obligations of both parties allocated in a single written document that includes the service level agreements and is available in a durable, accessible format [1].

At minimum, the contract must include:
- a clear and complete description of the functions and ICT services to be provided, including whether subcontracting is permitted and on what conditions [1];
- the locations where the services and data processing will take place, including storage locations, and advance notification if those locations change [1];
- provisions on availability, authenticity, integrity and confidentiality of data, including personal data [1];
- provisions ensuring access, recovery and return of personal and non-personal data in case of insolvency, resolution, discontinuation, or termination [2];
- service level descriptions, including updates and revisions [2];
- the ICT third-party service provider’s obligation to assist the financial entity at no additional cost, or at a cost fixed ex ante, when an ICT incident occurs [2];
- the obligation to fully cooperate with the competent and resolution authorities [2];
- termination rights and minimum notice periods, in line with competent and resolution authority expectations [2];
- conditions for participation in the financial entity’s ICT security awareness programmes and digital operational resilience training [2][6].

Where the services support critical or important functions, additional contractual terms are required, including:
- full service level descriptions with precise quantitative and qualitative performance targets for effective monitoring and corrective action [3];
- notice periods and reporting obligations, including notification of developments that may materially affect the provider’s ability to deliver the services [3];
- requirements to implement and test business contingency plans and maintain appropriate ICT security measures, tools and policies [3];
- detailed rights of access, inspection and audit, including the scope, procedures and frequency of inspections and audits [4];
- exit strategies, including a mandatory adequate transition period to reduce disruption and allow migration to another provider or in-house solutions [4].

The documents also say that financial entities should consider using standard contractual clauses developed by public authorities, and before contracting they must assess risks, due diligence, conflicts of interest, and whether appropriate information security standards are met [4][5].

The documents do not cover the exact wording each clause must use, nor do they provide a complete list beyond the contractual elements quoted above [1][2][3][4].
